//! lotus/stereo_echo.rye -- ALES181: the ECHO carried into stereo -- ALES66's feedback delay run on BOTH channels with the SAME delay and the SAME feedback, each channel reading and feeding back through ITS OWN LIVE buffer -- the FIRST rung of
//! the stereo TIME-BASED wing. The stereo MODULATION class is whole (ALES176-180: the amplitude pair -- tremolo, ring modulator -- beside the delay-line trio -- vibrato, chorus, flanger). ALES66 opened the mono time-based wing with its
//! primitive, the feedback delay y[i] = x[i] + fb-y[i-d]: each sample the dry input plus a fraction fb = num/den of the OUTPUT one delay earlier, so each echo feeds the next and the train decays. Carrying that primitive into stereo opens
//! the stereo delay wing exactly as ALES66 opened the mono one -- one primitive, then compositions -- and it is the natural crux to take next: the flanger (ALES180) already reads its own live output through a feedback path, so the echo, the
//! plain longer feedback delay the flanger's zero-depth case reduces to, is the foundation that whole wing already stands on. It is the SIMPLER live-feedback carry (a fixed whole delay, no LFO) and so the honest rung to seat the wing's base.
//!
//! The crux -- the SAME delay and feedback on BOTH channels, each on its OWN live buffer, is the linked stereo echo: both trains decay in lockstep, the image held. An echo's delay and feedback are a sample count and a named fraction the
//! caller NAMES, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the stereo image for free -- no linking machinery, no measurement. Mono `echo` reads the delayed OUTPUT off the clip buffer ITSELF (the
//! delay line IS the buffer), so run once per channel the left feeds back through the left's buffer and the right through the right's, never crossed -- NO cross-channel feedback, the two channels never hear each other. Because the delay is
//! at least one whole sample, `buf[i-delay]` sits strictly BEFORE the write index -- a sample already written this pass (true feedback), the dry audio before the span, or silence before the clip -- so an echo needs no carried state and a span
//! in two pieces equals the whole once, per channel. Feedback strictly below unity decays, so each echo is smaller than the last and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once, per channel. Because each channel's feedback loop stays
//! wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel (centred) master stays identical -- the image law the sense a mix cares about most. Honestly noted, like the flanger (ALES180) and unlike the snapshot rungs (vibrato, chorus), the echo
//! does NOT preserve an exact PANNED integer ratio: the feedback path accumulates `@divTrunc` truncation, so a right exactly half the left drifts from that ratio by ones once the loop has fed a few samples. That is honest and expected --
//! an accumulating feedback delay is not a linear scaler.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadDelay (a delay of zero, or wider than max_clip), BadFeedback (a zero denominator, or a feedback at or above unity -- a runaway that would never decay), and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE
//! either channel is touched -- the delay, feedback, and span validated ONCE against the shared length up front (ALES66's own `precheck`, factored out for this exactly as ALES73's was for the flanger), so a refusal never echoes one channel
//! and leaves the other torn; then run ALES66's `echo` on each channel with the SAME delay and feedback.
//!
//! The crux -- three laws. THE STEREO ECHO LAW: each channel equals ALES66's mono `echo` with the SAME delay and feedback over the same span byte for byte, proven side by side with genuinely different per-channel content -- the left rung by
//! the left's samples, the right by the right's, never crossed. THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW: a zero-feedback (fb_num = 0) stereo echo is the exact dry master on both channels -- no delayed voice added, the identity -- so the feedback genuinely
//! governs the train. THE IMAGE / BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an identical-channel (centred) master stays identical (each channel's feedback loop wholly within its own buffer); an all-silent master echoes to silence on
//! both; left.len == right.len after; a bad delay, a bad feedback, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both. Honestly noted: unlike the
//! snapshot rungs, the echo does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio (the feedback's `@divTrunc` truncation drifts it by ones), so the image law proven is the identical-channel one.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES181.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-085411_fill-ales181-lotus-stereo-echo.md
//! Stands on: lotus/echo.rye (ALES66) -- the mono feedback delay run once per channel, its EchoError reused whole and its `precheck` (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip
//! and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip and the one true saturate the mono echo reuses.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each rung through ALES66's own live-buffer feedback delay -- one delayed read and at most one multiply-divide per sample, run in i64 and each
//! channel reading and feeding back through its own buffer, the fed sum saturating once, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The delay is a count of sample INDICES, not
//! milliseconds against a clock; the feedback is a named fraction strictly below unity, not a decibel. No cross-channel feedback, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_echo.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const echo = @import("echo.rye");

pub const stereo_echo_version = "20260815.085411";

/// The stereo echo's faults are exactly ALES66's own `EchoError` -- `BadDelay`, `BadFeedback`, and `BadRange` -- reused by name, since
/// carrying the echo into stereo adds no new fault: it validates one shared delay, feedback, and span against the shared length, then
/// runs the same mono echo on each channel.
pub const StereoEchoError = echo.EchoError;

/// Apply a feedback echo over `count` samples starting at `start` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the SAME delay and feedback -- each sample
/// the dry input plus a fraction `fb_num/fb_den` of the OUTPUT one `delay` earlier -- by running ALES66's proven mono `echo` on each
/// channel. The shared delay and feedback preserve the stereo image: an identical-channel master stays identical (each channel's feedback
/// loop wholly within its own buffer). Each channel reads and feeds back through its OWN live buffer -- no cross-channel feedback. Validates
/// the delay, feedback, and span ONCE against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES66's `precheck`): refuses BadDelay,
/// BadFeedback, and BadRange, both channels untouched. Values only -- never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_echo(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    delay: u32,
    fb_num: u32,
    fb_den: u32,
) StereoEchoError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared delay and feedback preserve.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the delay, feedback, and span are legal against the SHARED length -- checked before any write, so a refusal never echoes one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    try echo.precheck(len, start, count, delay, fb_num, fb_den);

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels (same delay, same feedback, same span, shared length), so each mono echo is pre-validated to succeed -- no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME delay and
    // feedback on each channel, each reading and feeding back through its OWN live buffer, never the other's.
    try echo.echo(&sc.left, start, count, delay, fb_num, fb_den);
    try echo.echo(&sc.right, start, count, delay, fb_num, fb_den);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- an echo writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO ECHO LAW -- each channel equals ALES66's mono echo with the SAME delay and feedback byte for byte, different per-channel content; THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW -- a zero-feedback echo is the dry master on both; THE IMAGE /
//     BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- an identical-channel master staying identical, silence to silence, balanced, each of three refusals untouched, count 0 identity

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO ECHO LAW -- each channel equals ALES66's mono echo with the SAME delay and feedback, proven side by side over one span with genuinely different per-channel content: the left rung by the left's samples, the right by the
    //    right's, never crossed.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 8000, 0, 3000, 0, 5000, 0, 7000, 0 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ 0, 6000, 0, 2000, 0, 100, 0, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_echo(&sc, 0, 8, 2, 1, 2);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &ll);
        try echo.echo(&ml, 0, 8, 2, 1, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &rr);
        try echo.echo(&mr, 0, 8, 2, 1, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftEchoNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightEchoNotMono;
        // The two channels genuinely differ -- the left is not the right rung, proving no crossing.
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.ChannelsDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 2. THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW -- a zero-feedback (fb_num = 0) stereo echo adds nothing, the dry master passing byte for byte on both channels at any delay.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 1000, -2000, 3000, -4000, 5000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_echo(&sc, 0, 6, 3, 0, 4); // fb 0/4 — feedback fully off
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll)) return error.ZeroFbLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.ZeroFbRightChanged;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW (identical-channel) -- an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical through the echo: the same input through the same delay and feedback and the same live-buffer read is the same deterministic output
    //    on both channels, so a centred signal never splits. Like the flanger (ALES180) and unlike the snapshot rungs, the echo does NOT preserve an exact panned integer ratio (the feedback path's `@divTrunc` truncation drifts it by ones);
    //    the honest image law here is the identical-channel one -- the sense a mix cares about most (a centred voice stays centred).
    {
        const same = [_]i16{ 8000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &same, &same);
        try stereo_echo(&sc, 0, 8, 2, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 4a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- an all-silent master echoes to silence on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_echo(&sc, 0, 8, 2, 1, 2);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000, 3000, -3000, 100, -100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, 600, -600, 800, -800 });
        try stereo_echo(&bc, 0, 8, 3, 2, 3);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 8) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a bad delay (zero), a bad feedback (at or above unity -- a runaway), and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7, -8 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5, 4 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_echo(&a, 0, 4, 0, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadDelaySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDelay) return error.WrongBadDelay; // delay 0 — a sample cannot feed back into itself
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.DelayRefusalMutated;

        var f = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&f, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_echo(&f, 0, 4, 2, 4, 4)) |_| return error.BadFeedbackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFeedback) return error.WrongBadFeedback; // fb 4/4 = unity — a runaway
        if (!eq(f.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(f.right.samples(), &tr) or f.left.len != f.right.len) return error.FeedbackRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_echo(&d, 1, 4, 2, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange; // start 1 + count 4 > len 4
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.RangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no sample to echo.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_echo(&noop, 1, 0, 2, 1, 2); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-echo: the ECHO CARRIED INTO STEREO — ALES66's feedback delay run on BOTH channels with the SAME delay and feedback (y[i] = saturate(x[i] + fb·y[i-delay])), each channel reading and feeding back through ITS " ++
        "OWN LIVE buffer, the FIRST rung of the stereo TIME-BASED wing (the stereo MODULATION class whole at ALES176–180). ALES66 opened the mono time-based wing with this primitive; carrying it into stereo opens the stereo delay wing " ++
        "the same way — one primitive, then compositions — and it is the natural crux next: the flanger (ALES180) already reads its own live output through a feedback path, and the echo is the plain longer feedback delay that whole wing " ++
        "stands on, the simpler live-feedback carry (a fixed whole delay, no LFO). The SAME delay and feedback on both channels, each on its OWN live buffer, is the linked stereo echo: both trains decay in lockstep, the image held. The " ++
        "delay is a sample count and the feedback a named fraction the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the image for free; mono echo reads the delayed OUTPUT off the " ++
        "clip buffer ITSELF, so run once per channel the left feeds back through the left's buffer and the right the right's, never crossed — no cross-channel feedback, and because the delay is at least one whole sample buf[i-delay] sits " ++
        "strictly before the write index (an already-written output, the dry audio, or silence), so an echo needs no carried state. Feedback below unity decays and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once per channel; because each " ++
        "channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel master stays identical. Honestly noted: like the flanger and unlike the snapshot rungs, the echo does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, " ++
        "the feedback's truncation drifting it by ones (expected of an accumulating feedback delay). It validates the delay, feedback, and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadDelay, BadFeedback, " ++
        "BadRange; ALES66's precheck reused), so a refusal never echoes one channel and leaves the other torn; EchoError reused whole. THE STEREO ECHO LAW: each channel equals ALES66's mono echo with the same delay and feedback byte for " ++
        "byte, the channels genuinely differing. THE DRY-IDENTITY LAW: a zero-feedback echo is the dry master on both. THE IMAGE / BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an identical-channel master staying identical, silence to " ++
        "silence on both, left.len == right.len after, a bad delay / a bad feedback / an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 " ++
        "Clips, each rung through the proven mono echo's live-buffer feedback delay, the delay a count of sample indices not milliseconds and the feedback a named fraction below unity not a decibel; no cross-channel feedback, no real " ++
        "sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
